ment": -2.41, "key_factors": ["Open seat in an R-leaning district (PVI R+4) with Golden not on the ballot", "Most expert raters call it Lean R for a flip; Kalshi prices LePage ahead", "Recent polls are mostly Democratic-sponsored and show a near-even race", "LePage leads in cash on hand; residency attacks are a Democratic angle"], "rationale": "The polling average actually shows Dunlap narrowly ahead, but it is dominated by Democratic-sponsored surveys, while six of ten expert raters call the seat Lean R for a flip and Kalshi prices LePage at about a two-thirds chance. Weighing the R+4 fundamentals and the rating consensus against the thin, sponsor-skewed polling puts LePage modestly ahead.", "watch": "A credible nonpartisan poll breaking for Dunlap, or Kalshi repricing toward even, would move this back to a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Jared Golden (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Maine's 2nd District is an open seat with Jared Golden retiring, and the contest pairs former two-term governor Paul LePage against former Secretary of State Matt Dunlap. The district leans Republican by about four points, and the national environment favors the GOP, though Golden's past wins show Democrats can carry it.\n\nRecent surveys put the race essentially even, with Dunlap slightly ahead in the latest polls, but most of those come from Democratic-aligned pollsters. Six of ten expert raters call the race Lean R for a flip, while others rate it a tossup. LePage holds a cash-on-hand edge, and prediction markets lean his way. The race sits on a knife's edge heading into the final stretch.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4239, "margin": -1.15}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.38, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3812, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.377, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3179, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.4062, "margin": -1.0}, {"date"